 1 big thing: Democrats&#x27; blue border wall 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios. Photos: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Voters handed Democrats every House district touching Mexico and all four border-state governorships.

 Why it matters: Starting next year, the sweep gives Democrats new leverage over state resources, border spending and legal challenges during President Trump's final two years, Axios' Russell Contreras reports. 🧮 By the numbers: Democrats last held all four governorships — California, Arizona, New Mexico and Texas — in January 1979, according to National Governors Association records . 

 Democrats swept all 10 House districts touching Mexico for the first time since 2008, per an AP analysis . Three were flips. Democrats also won the top legal offices in all four border states, including Texas , where the party hadn't won an attorney general race since 1994. Zoom in: Texas Democrat Gina Hinojosa's defeat of Gov. Greg Abbott (R) could bring the most immediate border changes.

 Abbott spent about $11 billion on Operation Lone Star, deploying state police and National Guard troops and building Rio Grande barriers, The Texas Tribune reports . Hinojosa, Texas' first Latina governor-elect, has questioned that spending. She can rethink state deployments and enforcement priorities, though existing laws and federal authority limit her options. 🔭 Zoom out: Several Democrats elected in border districts campaigned on both strong border security and pathways to citizenship for longtime undocumented immigrants.

 Bobby Pulido, who flipped a Texas GOP-held seat, said in his launch video : "Yes, we have to get the criminals out and secure the border. But we can do that without destroying families and our South Texas economy." He also told The Texas Tribune that he wants to codify a pathway to citizenship. JoAnna Mendoza, who flipped an Arizona seat, also backs a strong border and legal pathways to citizenship. Share this story . 

 
 
