 &nbsp; 
 
 
 2. 💰 Nvidia scandal hits 401(k)s 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Nvidia's near-30% stock plunge in the past month is a drag on the retirement savings of millions of Americans.

 Why it matters: Nvidia's enormous weight in popular index funds means even those who have never purchased a single share of the company could feel the pain of its corporate upheaval in their 401(k)s, Axios' Madison Mills reports. 📉 By the numbers: Index funds held nearly half of the assets in large private-sector 401(k) plans in 2023, according to the Investment Company Institute. 

 Those funds include investments tracking the S&amp;P 500, and Nvidia accounts for roughly 8% of that index, according to Tom Graff, chief investment officer at financial planning firm Facet. That exposure rises to around 14% in growth-style index funds and can reach 25% to 30% in some technology and semiconductor funds commonly found in retirement plans, Graff said. 🔎 Zoom in : For an investor with $100,000 in an S&amp;P 500 fund, a 30% drop in Nvidia shares would knock about $2,400 off the value of that investment given an 8% S&amp;P holding, assuming other stocks in the fund stayed flat.

 For a growth-style tech fund with 25% exposure to Nvidia, that decline would amount to $7,500 in lost value. Keep reading . 

 
 
