 3. ✈️ FAA: Air travel should normalize today 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Travelers waiting in line at a ticket counter at JFK Airport in New York yesterday. Photo: Ryan Murphy/Getty Images The U.S. air travel schedule should return to normal by the end of today, two days after the nation's longest ground stop in 25 years, Federal Aviation Administration Administrator Bryan Bedford told reporters yesterday. 

 U.S. flights had 9,201 cancellations on Friday, 6,692 yesterday and 1,445 so far today, according to the FlightAware tracker . At airports across America yesterday, passengers were still scrambling to reach their destinations. Some waited out the chaos in hotels. Others sprinted to snag rental cars and bus or train tickets. Many camped out in airports. Transportation Secretary Sean Duffy explained the outage was caused when a contractor upgraded a telecommunications copper wire to fiber-optic cable and installed a new router.

 "It was such an old wire that it was being discontinued, meaning that line was going to get shut down. So we had to switch it over from old copper," Duffy said. He said teams are working to figure out whether the outage occurred because of contractor error or faulty equipment. 📺  Yesterday, CNN had a tracker in a corner of the screen showing FlightAware's tally of delays and cancellations. As Duffy spoke, each figure rose relentlessly.

