 6. 🤖 Crowded skies 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Sprawling networks of space data centers could make it nearly impossible for humans to stargaze.

 That's just one of the many risks that come with placing tens of thousands of AI computers into orbit, Axios' Thomas Novelly writes from a new report for the Innovation for Cool Earth Forum. Why it matters: Most Americans don't want data centers built near them, but putting them in space brings a new set of problems.

 🌟 If space data centers proliferate, the "most visible points of light in the sky during much of the night in much of the world could be satellites, not stars," the report said, mirroring astronomers ' worries. 🪖 If the U.S. military adopts the technology, the data centers could become targets . 💥 The risk of collisions grows as thousands of orbital data centers whip around the Earth at 17,000 mph. 🚀 Space data centers are solar-powered. But the rocket launches needed to put them in orbit generate greenhouse gases and black carbon emissions . By the numbers: Despite those concerns, investors and tech innovators are all in on space data centers.

 In 2025, around $197 million in investments were tied to orbital data centers, per market research firm Quilty Space. That figure has swelled to $1.11 billion so far this year. Go deeper . 

 
 
